"q-162476" :question/id #uuid "335f364d-6d48-59d7-8861-1972f730d325" :question/math-academy-id "q-162476" :question/problem "Find the radius of the curvature for the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n$R(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}.$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,y(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))-(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))|}{{((2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))^{2}+(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-8{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)-8{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)|}{{(4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+4{sin}^{2}⁡(2t))}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{1}{κ(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-162476/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36986a18-172c-5983-8f6f-3211b6e8f89d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-162476"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-162526/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "3d2b3198-fe9f-58db-a0db-d01366774920" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162526/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "608170bc-1ad3-5ced-8bfd-0c39f1c40498" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162526/selection/e014eef6d5b5b99f76a63badf4d7e4589d1524a951e6f15c41809dcf478e3da9" :answer/id #uuid "20af2c31-0df2-581a-8aa5-69048bac977e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162526/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "e7555779-dd38-500d-9342-951c89bdbb45" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162526/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "55e4cadd-d138-5493-9838-7eccadbf0a4e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162526/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "e65c04fe-056c-559e-831f-62a5b6a593e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-162526/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-162526/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-162526/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-162526/selection/e014eef6d5b5b99f76a63badf4d7e4589d1524a951e6f15c41809dcf478e3da9" "q-162526/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-162526/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-162526" :question/id #uuid "7692858e-e7c4-5025-bcf9-d2dc03758f14" :question/math-academy-id "q-162526" :question/problem "Find the curvature of the curve $y=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ at $x=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(x)$ of a plane curve $y=y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(x)=\\frac{|{y}^{″}(x)|}{(1+[{y}^{′}(x)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${y}^{′}(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\, & {y}^{″}(x) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(x) & =\\frac{|2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|}{{[1+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{2}]}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $x=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{|2\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-0|}{{(1+0)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-162526/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "384072a8-f008-5279-bb58-8086eb42b710"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-162526"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-35190"] :question/problem "Find the signed curvature of the curve $y=2{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+1$ at $x=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The signed curvature $k(x)$ of a plane curve $y=y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(x)=\\frac{{y}^{″}(x)}{(1+[{y}^{′}(x)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${y}^{′}(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=6{x}^{2}-2x,\\,{y}^{″}(x)=12x-2\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the signed curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(x) & =\\frac{12x-2}{{[1+{(6{x}^{2}-2x)}^{2}]}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $x=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(0) & =\\frac{12(0)-2}{{[1+{(6\\cdot {0}^{2}-2\\cdot 0)}^{2}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{{1}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-162525/selection/b0c347139c7763e004d0e217791c28671593c0d4316e9f720ea33f2096e3aa3d" :answer/id #uuid "88b39bc6-1287-552e-b95f-04e82975189d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{20}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162525/selection/e144d0c9a4b5dee785495226d2f478686a88fc75da472f4079fae10f35f77cc1" :answer/id #uuid "efae2038-ef4c-57ef-889a-527e252ffc29" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{25}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162525/selection/c060e83d07bbf3a58eb77215c3232bcaac9d5b1a4f23c213779e0f28c54fe2a2" :answer/id #uuid "8cb98ae0-e0fc-5ccc-a358-0b0fda139f54" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{50}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162525/selection/279f97a4caf24c49cd23373145e395b747560d810238ce82ecdafa0f598cfb1a" :answer/id #uuid "4bf25ff9-66dd-5a44-a7d4-031d13f59985" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{50}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162525/selection/fea58e19e21853e9172747980c34f35237f689582f9c2617190bac98fba4d1aa" :answer/id #uuid "1c22991a-9f29-5d82-9d67-847a2134b7b3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162525/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "64121242-e881-539d-8b78-0a477dae4d9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-162525/selection/279f97a4caf24c49cd23373145e395b747560d810238ce82ecdafa0f598cfb1a" :answer-field/choices ["q-162525/selection/b0c347139c7763e004d0e217791c28671593c0d4316e9f720ea33f2096e3aa3d" "q-162525/selection/e144d0c9a4b5dee785495226d2f478686a88fc75da472f4079fae10f35f77cc1" "q-162525/selection/c060e83d07bbf3a58eb77215c3232bcaac9d5b1a4f23c213779e0f28c54fe2a2" "q-162525/selection/279f97a4caf24c49cd23373145e395b747560d810238ce82ecdafa0f598cfb1a" "q-162525/selection/fea58e19e21853e9172747980c34f35237f689582f9c2617190bac98fba4d1aa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-162525" :question/id #uuid "e448f92d-acb3-51b2-99d6-b710840c0ae3" :question/math-academy-id "q-162525" :question/problem "Find the curvature of the curve $y={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}$ at $x=-1.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(x)$ of a plane curve $y=y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(x)=\\frac{|{y}^{″}(x)|}{(1+[{y}^{′}(x)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${y}^{′}(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}-4x,\\, & {y}^{″}(x) & =6x-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(x) & =\\frac{|6x-4|}{{[1+{(3{x}^{2}-4x)}^{2}]}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $x=-1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(-1) & =\\frac{|6(-1)-4|}{{(1+{(3(-1)^{2}-4(-1))}^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-10|}{{(1+{7}^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{{(50)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{50\\sqrt{50}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{50}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-162525/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "384072a8-f008-5279-bb58-8086eb42b710"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-162525"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-34549"] :question/problem "Find the curvature of the curve $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)$ at $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(x)$ of a plane curve $y=y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(x)=\\frac{|{y}^{″}(x)|}{(1+[{y}^{′}(x)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${y}^{′}(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{2}{x-1},\\, & {y}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{2}{(x-1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(x) & =\\frac{|-\\frac{2}{(x-1)^{2}}|}{{[1+{(\\frac{2}{x-1})}^{2}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(x-1)^{2}{[1+\\frac{4}{(x-1)^{2}}]}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $x=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(2) & =\\frac{2}{(2-1)^{2}{[1+\\frac{4}{(2-1)^{2}}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{5}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-162534/selection/6a78441cadc7c63b36803de448939469a5c6aad4016273c13650c8522a085cf3" :answer/id #uuid "9794adc2-8663-5aa4-a0cf-8623185dc810" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2{ln}^{2}⁡2}{(2+{ln}^{2}⁡2)^{3/2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162534/selection/086d2d4b1e169a524c72bfe88cb9358c48bf69f2046da93a0637e4f2244c4459" :answer/id #uuid "b7820ce4-ec69-55d5-b3c5-78a68055b77c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{8{ln}^{2}⁡2}{(4+{ln}^{2}⁡2)^{3/2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162534/selection/f3a63f400ae1f0ad17f00c6b2b4f639c4aed1698ab5d93bfa8927a5f8789bcdb" :answer/id #uuid "d6f87ae4-1dfe-5dad-9c1b-8b88e34cb4b4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4{ln}^{2}⁡2}{(4+{ln}^{2}⁡2)^{3/2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162534/selection/e3b9d0aef42844484d877fdbc3b8c6e848a224af7f546d0f4a24f0d154a0d41e" :answer/id #uuid "ce26222c-742b-5a86-8122-8ba32a0afbd4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{2{ln}^{2}⁡2}{(1+{ln}^{2}⁡2)^{3/2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162534/selection/8f287f5df22c74764027daec3cae1f73aa03ed47058471ce6a2aaef8cd25245b" :answer/id #uuid "2def3a6b-9d19-58a7-9867-721ac3f0839d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4{ln}^{2}⁡2}{(1+{ln}^{2}⁡2)^{3/2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162534/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "1b69b343-8f2b-56de-909b-666ce2bb1a4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-162534/selection/f3a63f400ae1f0ad17f00c6b2b4f639c4aed1698ab5d93bfa8927a5f8789bcdb" :answer-field/choices ["q-162534/selection/6a78441cadc7c63b36803de448939469a5c6aad4016273c13650c8522a085cf3" "q-162534/selection/086d2d4b1e169a524c72bfe88cb9358c48bf69f2046da93a0637e4f2244c4459" "q-162534/selection/f3a63f400ae1f0ad17f00c6b2b4f639c4aed1698ab5d93bfa8927a5f8789bcdb" "q-162534/selection/e3b9d0aef42844484d877fdbc3b8c6e848a224af7f546d0f4a24f0d154a0d41e" "q-162534/selection/8f287f5df22c74764027daec3cae1f73aa03ed47058471ce6a2aaef8cd25245b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-162534" :question/id #uuid "c73664f3-05a3-5a66-826a-ecc745ac4006" :question/math-academy-id "q-162534" :question/problem "Find the signed curvature of the curve $y={2}^{x}$ at $x=-1.$" :question/worked-solution "The signed curvature $k(x)$ of a plane curve $y=y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(x)=\\frac{{y}^{″}(x)}{(1+[{y}^{′}(x)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${y}^{′}(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)={2}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,{y}^{″}(x)={2}^{x}{ln}^{2}⁡2\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the signed curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(x) & =\\frac{{2}^{x}{ln}^{2}⁡2}{{[1+{({2}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}^{2}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{x}{ln}^{2}⁡2}{{(1+{2}^{2x}{ln}^{2}⁡2)}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $x=-1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(-1) & =\\frac{{2}^{-1}{ln}^{2}⁡2}{{(1+{2}^{2(-1)}{ln}^{2}⁡2)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡2}{2})}{{(\\frac{4+{ln}^{2}⁡2}{4})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{ln}^{2}⁡2}{(4+{ln}^{2}⁡2)^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-162534/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "384072a8-f008-5279-bb58-8086eb42b710"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-162534"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3454"] :question/problem "The signed curvature of the plane curve $\\mathbf r(t) = \\big\\langle t, \\: \\sin t \\big\\rangle$ is given by\n\n$$\nk (t) = \\dfrac{\\bbox[2pt, border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}}{\\sqrt{ (1+\\cos^2 t)^3 } }, \\quad t\\neq 0.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" :question/worked-solution "The signed curvature $k(t)$ of a plane curve $\\mathbf r(t) =  \\left \\langle x(t), y(t) \\right \\rangle$ is given by\n\n$$\nk (t) = \\dfrac {x'(t) y''(t) - y'(t) x''(t)} {\\big([x'(t)]^2 + [y'(t)]^2\\big)^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t) = t, \\qquad y(t) = \\sin t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =1,\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =0,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the signed curvature, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(t) & =\\frac{1\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{{({1}^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{{(1+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\sqrt{(1+{cos}^{2}⁡t)^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3452"] :question/problem "Find the radius of curvature for the curve  $\\mathbf r(t) =  t \\, \\mathbf i + \\sin t\\, \\mathbf j$ at $t = \\dfrac \\pi 2.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $\\kappa(t)$ of a plane curve $\\mathbf r(t) = x(t) \\, \\mathbf i + y(t)\\,\\mathbf j$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\kappa (t) = \\dfrac {|x'(t) y''(t) - y'(t) x''(t)|} {\\big([x'(t)]^2 + [y'(t)]^2\\big)^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n$R(t) = \\dfrac 1 {\\kappa(t)}.$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t) = t, \\qquad y(t) = \\sin t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =1,\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =0,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|1\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 0|}{{({1}^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\operatorname{sin}⁡t|}{{(1+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t = \\dfrac{\\pi}2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})|}{{(1+{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{(1+{0}^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $t = \\dfrac{\\pi}2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{1}{κ(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3453"] :question/problem "Find the curvature of the curve $y = x^3$ at $x=-1.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $\\kappa(x)$ of a plane curve $y = y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\kappa(x) = \\dfrac {|y''(x)|} {(1+[y'(x)]^2)^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $y'(x)$ and $y''(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2},\\, & {y}^{″}(x) & =6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(x) & =\\frac{|6x|}{{[1+{(3{x}^{2})}^{2}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|6x|}{{(1+9{x}^{4})}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $x=-1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(-1) & =\\frac{|6(-1)|}{{(1+9(-1)^{4})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-6|}{{(1+9)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{{10}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{10\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{50}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
